Q: How can I run clang-tidy on all the cpp files in my project in a performant way? A: <p>As <code>clang-tidy</code> is single-threaded and processes one file after another, you can run multiple instances – one per CPU – in parallel:</p>
<pre class="lang-bash prettyprint-override"><code>#!/bin/sh
set -e -u

die () { printf '%s\n' &quot;$@&quot; &gt;&amp;2; exit 1; }
have () { command -v &quot;${1:?}&quot; &gt;/dev/null 2&gt;&amp;1; }

have clang-tidy || die &quot;Missing executable 'clang-tidy'&quot;
have jq || die &quot;Missing executable 'jq'&quot;
have xargs || die &quot;Missing executable 'xargs'&quot;

cc=&quot;${1:-compile_commands.json}&quot;
[ -f &quot;$cc&quot; ] || die &quot;File not found: $cc&quot;

tmp=$(mktemp -d)
cleanup () { rm -rf &quot;$tmp&quot;; }
trap cleanup EXIT

jq -r '.[]|if (.file|startswith(&quot;/&quot;)) then .file else (.directory+&quot;/&quot;+.file) end' &quot;$cc&quot; &gt;&quot;${tmp}/files&quot;
# clang-tidy &quot;@${tmp}/files&quot;

cpus=&quot;$(nproc)&quot;
lines=&quot;$(wc -l &lt;&quot;${tmp}/files&quot;)&quot;
export cc tmp
xargs \
  --arg-file=&quot;${tmp}/files&quot; \
  --delimiter='\n' \
  --no-run-if-empty \
  --max-args=&quot;$((1 + (lines - 1) / cpus))&quot; \
  --max-procs=&quot;$cpus&quot; \
  --process-slot-var=slot \
  sh -c 'clang-tidy -p &quot;$cc&quot; &quot;$@&quot; &gt;&quot;${tmp}/${slot}&quot; &amp;&amp; flock --exclusive 1 &amp;&amp; cat &quot;${tmp}/${slot}&quot;'
</code></pre>
<p>Output must be serialized as otherwise the output of multiple processes writing to the same STDOUT will get mangled.</p>
<p>The scheduling may not be optimal as the list of files is distributes before-hand in large chunks to the individual <code>clang-tidy</code>-processes: One may get all the small files, quit early and be idle afterwards, while another one might all the large files and be the one taking longest.</p>
<p>You can &quot;fix&quot; this by setting <code>--max-args=2</code> (or some other small number); that way you'll have to <code>fork+exec(clang-tidy)</code> more often, but each one will check fewer files and the above mention problem will become more unlikely.</p>
<p>Optimal would be to run the slowest first, but that seldom correlates to the file-size as <code>#include</code>s may drastically change that.</p>
